# Baked-in parameter defaults for NXP MR-VMU-RT1176.
#
# Embedded into ROMFS at build time and applied via HAL_PARAM_DEFAULTS_PATH.
#
# These are compulsory parameters with no meaningful built-in default, so they
# belong here rather than being special-cased in vehicle code. Doing it here
# also means the value is present on the very first boot, before any GCS has
# connected - which is essential on this board while persistent storage is
# still being brought up, because parameters are currently RAM-only and do not
# survive a reboot.

# Frame configuration. ArduCopter's allocate_motors() runs before ins.init(),
# and a frame with no motors allocated is a fatal
# AP_BoardConfig::allocation_error(), so this has to be right from boot.
#   FRAME_CLASS 1 = Quad
#   FRAME_TYPE  1 = X
FRAME_CLASS 1
FRAME_TYPE 1

# EKF3: ONE filter core, not two.
#
# HAL_EKF_IMU_MASK_DEFAULT is 3 - "Default to using two IMUs" - so EKF3
# instantiates TWO complete NavEKF3_core instances and runs both every update.
# EKF3 is the dominant cost in the main loop here: @SYS/tasks.txt puts
# Copter::read_AHRS at ~63% of the loop with two cores, and the ekf3_update
# phase marker accounts for ~9-10 ms of it per iteration against a 2.5 ms
# budget for 400 Hz.
#
# Only ONE IMU needs to be fast to fly, so a second filter lane is
# redundancy this board cannot currently afford. Halving the
# filter work attacks the single largest consumer directly.
#
# MEASURED on hardware 2026-08-07, two independent trials per arm, each a
# 150 s time series sampled in 2 s bins (Tools/zephyr/zephyr_timeseries.py):
#
#   EK3_IMU_MASK 3 (two cores)   ~53 Hz median main loop
#   EK3_IMU_MASK 1 (one core)     62.9 / 63.0 Hz median, 0/75 stalled bins
#
# After the change read_AHRS falls to 49.5% of the loop and ekf3_update to
# ~5.4 ms. It remains the largest single consumer, so this reduces the cost
# rather than removing it.
#
# Set here rather than as a stored parameter because parameter storage on this
# board is RAM-only and does not survive a reboot; defaults.parm is baked into
# ROMFS and applies from the very first boot.
EK3_IMU_MASK 1

# Fast rate thread (ArduCopter/rate_thread.cpp): gyro -> filter -> rate
# control -> motors in a dedicated high-priority thread, independent of the
# main loop. Enabled 2026-08-10 ahead of RCOutput/motors being
# hardware-verified on this port; motor bench testing is deferred, and the
# paragraph below is why that is safe.
#
# Safe to enable without rcout->init() having run: hal.rcout->write()/
# set_dshot_rate()/force_trigger_groups() all silently no-op until
# RCOutput::_map_ready is set, which only happens inside RCOutput::init()
# (verified by reading RCOutput.cpp - _apply_channel() bails immediately if
# !_map_ready). No PWM device is touched and no motor can spin from this
# alone; ArduCopter's arming state machine is a separate, unmet gate anyway
# (PreArm: RC not found / accel cal needed / compass unhealthy all still
# fail on this bench).
#
# 1 = Enabled-Dynamic: divisor auto-scales from FSTRATE_DIV to avoid gyro
# buffer overrun / main loop slowdown, rather than forcing a fixed rate.
#
# HW-VERIFIED COST 2026-08-10: main loop settles to 429-439 Hz with the rate
# thread live (was 496-510 Hz without it) - the rate thread runs at a higher
# priority than the main loop by design (PRIORITY_RCOUT+1, Zephyr prio 3,
# above APM_MAIN_PRIORITY(8)) and genuinely competes for CPU. Still clear of
# the 400 Hz floor, but with less margin than before - worth
# rechecking after any future main-loop-cost change.
FSTRATE_ENABLE 1

# Serial RC input port. SERIAL7 is USART6 in this board's SERIAL_ORDER.
#
# AP_HAL_Zephyr's RCInput::init() used to hand hal.serial(7) straight to
# AP_RCProtocol::add_uart(). That ran before AP_SerialManager::init(), so it
# always won the single RCIN uart slot and any SERIALn_PROTOCOL 23 set on a
# different port was refused with "duplicate RCIN not permitted". The call is
# gone; this parameter reproduces the old wiring the way every ChibiOS board
# does it, and leaves the port selectable.
#   23 = RCIN
# CAUTION (2026-10-12): on the current airframe the ELRS receiver is on SERIAL2
# (TELEM2), which is also 23. Two ports at 23 is "PreArm: Multiple SERIAL ports
# configured for RC in", so the vehicle holds SERIAL7_PROTOCOL -1. After any
# reset-to-defaults, set SERIAL7_PROTOCOL -1 again (or change this line).
SERIAL7_PROTOCOL 23

# Main loop rate. The old hwdef default of 600 Hz was measured on 2026-08-08 on
# a lighter build; by 2026-10-12 the same board achieved 275 Hz against it with
# the CPU 100% busy and 0% idle. When the loop cannot be met, the INS wait never
# sleeps, so main never gives the lower-priority threads anything beyond the
# per-loop yield - the same thing a ChibiOS board would do if overloaded, but
# ChibiOS boards are not overloaded at 400 Hz. The result here was the I2C
# compass and baros reporting Bad Health (successful transfers taking
# 85-122 ms of wall time), MAVFTP unusable, and "AP_Logger: stuck thread
# (fsync)" from one io_timer() call that took 22.8 s.
#
# 300 is the bench-measured value that removed all of that: loop ~275-345 Hz,
# 0% I2C unhealthy. With the fast rate thread (FSTRATE_ENABLE 1 above) the
# attitude rate loop runs at gyro rate regardless of this number. Raise it only
# after PM.NLon shows no long loops at the higher rate.
SCHED_LOOP_RATE 300

# ESC update rate. Two of the ESCs fitted in the repairs after flight attempt 3
# accept only 50 Hz PWM. AP_MotorsMatrix::init() applies ONE rate to the whole
# motor mask (rc_set_freq(mask, _speed_hz), fed from this parameter), so there
# is no per-motor rate: all four run at the slowest ESC's rate.
#
# Reachable with the existing FlexPWM setup, checked rather than assumed:
# nxp,prescaler is 128, giving the 1.875 MHz counter tick the register dump
# measured, so 20 ms is 37,500 counts against the 16-bit limit of 65,535.
# pwm_mcux.c refuses any period over UINT16_MAX and cannot change the
# prescaler at runtime, so the floor on this board is about 28.6 Hz.
#
# Cost: the Copter default is 490 Hz. At 50 Hz a motor command only reaches
# the ESC every 20 ms, which is a real reduction in attitude-control
# bandwidth. Revisit when those two ESCs are replaced.
RC_SPEED 50

# Throttle failsafe OFF. Bench setting, at the maintainer's request (2026-10-12):
# with the transmitter off, an ELRS receiver in failsafe streams frozen stick
# values and the vehicle would otherwise act on them during bench work. With
# this at 0 the FC cannot tell "pilot holding centre sticks" from "transmitter
# dead", so it MUST be 1 before anything flies. Baked in here because
# parameter sets do not get through while the board is saturated, and a
# reset-to-defaults is the planned way back to a known state.
FS_THR_ENABLE 0
